  "statement": "The sequence $a_{n}$ satisfies $a_{1}=2$ and $a_{n+1}=3a_{n}-2$. (i) Find $a_{2},a_{3},a_{4}$. (ii) Prove by induction that $a_{n}=3^{n-1}+1$ for all $n\\ge 1$. (iii) Hence find $\\sum_{k=1}^{n}a_{k}$ in closed form.",
  "solution": "(i) $a_{2}=3\\cdot2-2=4$, $a_{3}=3\\cdot4-2=10$, $a_{4}=3\\cdot10-2=28$. (ii) Base $a_{1}=2=3^{0}+1$. Assume $a_{n}=3^{n-1}+1$; then $a_{n+1}=3(3^{n-1}+1)-2=3^{n}+1$, as required. (iii) $\\sum_{k=1}^{n}a_{k}=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}(3^{k-1}+1)=\\frac{3^{n}-1}{3-1}+n=\\frac{3^{n}-1}{2}+n$.",
